# Cloudflare reads this from the deployed asset directory and does not serve it.
#
# A document's numbered sections used to be prerendered pages of their own
# (`/documents/{slug}/{n}` — 9,315 files, 61% of the whole deployment, each
# carrying a few hundred bytes of text behind ~21 KB of repeated chrome). A
# document is now one page and a section is a fragment on it; see
# docs/decisions.md, 2026-08-17.
#
# Nothing generates the old form any more, and nothing outside this repo has
# ever linked to it — the site had been public for a day when the routes
# merged. This rule exists so the old, guessable shape still lands correctly if
# anyone types it or has it in a note. ONE dynamic rule covers all 9,315 of
# them, against Cloudflare's limit of 100; the destination fragment is applied
# by the browser after the redirect.
#
# `:n` IS A PLACEHOLDER, NOT A NUMBER MATCHER, so it also swallows the literal
# `read` of the equally-retired continuous view: `/documents/{slug}/read`
# resolves to `/documents/{slug}#sread`. That is harmless — it lands on the
# correct page and the nonsense fragment matches no element, so the reader
# arrives at the top — and it is left alone deliberately. A more specific
# `/documents/:slug/read` rule placed above this one does NOT win: Cloudflare
# does not resolve between two DYNAMIC rules by file order (only static-before-
# dynamic and same-source-path ordering are documented, and neither applies).
# Tested against the live deployment before being removed again, so nobody
# needs to re-run that experiment.
/documents/:slug/:n  /documents/:slug#s:n  301
